1
00:00:00,059 --> 00:00:03,950
Reports brings several questions
together: who is currently staffed,

2
00:00:04,219 --> 00:00:07,739
what happened on calls, and which
timestamps have been recorded.

3
00:00:08,229 --> 00:00:12,310
In our fictional Chicago Fire department,
begin with the Reporting catalog.

4
00:00:12,810 --> 00:00:15,979
Find Staffing Report and
use its View Report button.

5
00:00:16,479 --> 00:00:19,770
The report groups people by their
latest recorded staffing level

6
00:00:19,809 --> 00:00:21,489
and provides a count and chart.

7
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:26,889
Truck 81 has people in Available,
Unavailable and On Shift categories.

8
00:00:27,219 --> 00:00:30,459
This is a staffing summary;
it does not establish who

9
00:00:30,459 --> 00:00:32,209
physically attended an incident.

10
00:00:32,399 --> 00:00:36,839
Below the summary, read Name,
Group, Roles, Staffing and

11
00:00:36,839 --> 00:00:38,570
Staffing Timestamp together.

12
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:43,790
Next Change and Next Staffing describe
a scheduled change when one exists.

13
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:47,030
A blank cell supplies no
future staffing commitment.

14
00:00:47,519 --> 00:00:50,910
Wallace Boden’s row also carries
an explicit training note.

15
00:00:51,399 --> 00:00:55,910
Farther down, Matthew Casey’s row
shows Truck 81, Officer and the

16
00:00:55,910 --> 00:00:59,949
training Community Response Navigator
role, with On Shift staffing.

17
00:01:00,399 --> 00:01:04,459
The report reads existing personnel
information; running it does not

18
00:01:04,509 --> 00:01:06,509
edit those memberships or statuses.

19
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:11,949
On the Call Summary Report parameter
page, check Report Start Time, Report End

20
00:01:11,949 --> 00:01:14,869
Time and Group before using Run Report.

21
00:01:15,299 --> 00:01:18,610
This example starts October
twenty-fifth, twenty twenty-six,

22
00:01:18,669 --> 00:01:22,440
at midnight and ends October
twenty-ninth at twenty-three thirty.

23
00:01:22,859 --> 00:01:24,320
All Groups is selected.

24
00:01:24,779 --> 00:01:27,129
These are department-local
report boundaries.

25
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:32,680
Read the complete values before running:
a different start, end or group changes

26
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,369
the population being summarized.

27
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,220
The resulting header
repeats the selected period.

28
00:01:37,649 --> 00:01:42,130
This captured run contains fifteen
calls, with four open, nine unit

29
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:44,860
responses and four personnel responses.

30
00:01:45,249 --> 00:01:49,639
Response totals accumulate across
calls; they are not a count of unique

31
00:01:49,639 --> 00:01:51,459
department members or vehicles.

32
00:01:51,859 --> 00:01:54,100
The tables provide a useful cross-check.

33
00:01:54,459 --> 00:01:59,100
Investigation, Structure Fire, Rescue
and Medical counts total fifteen.

34
00:01:59,449 --> 00:02:03,060
Call States separates eleven
Closed from four Active.

35
00:02:03,449 --> 00:02:06,430
Read the table labels and
denominators before comparing

36
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:08,159
percentages with another report.

37
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:12,010
Calls by Group explains why a
call belongs to a reporting group.

38
00:02:12,459 --> 00:02:14,010
Each call counts once.

39
00:02:14,410 --> 00:02:19,219
A containing group geofence is used
first; otherwise the report uses the group

40
00:02:19,219 --> 00:02:21,870
associated with most dispatched resources.

41
00:02:22,269 --> 00:02:24,179
Some calls can remain unassigned.

42
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,570
This is different from simply
listing the current viewer’s group.

43
00:02:27,990 --> 00:02:32,180
Here, Firehouse 51 has seven
calls and Command has five.

44
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:36,649
Use the explanation when investigating
a surprising group total instead

45
00:02:36,649 --> 00:02:40,010
of changing memberships to make
a report match expectations.

46
00:02:40,210 --> 00:02:43,360
The Calls table lets you inspect
the rows behind the summary.

47
00:02:43,769 --> 00:02:50,250
Read Number, Name, Type, Group, Logged
On, Closed On and the duration columns.

48
00:02:50,710 --> 00:02:53,800
The original three DEMO calls
are still Open in this result,

49
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:56,050
and their OnScene Time is N/A.

50
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:01,010
The lower rows include the native Unit
response and deselection exercise,

51
00:03:01,299 --> 00:03:05,910
number ending zero zero zero eight,
also Open at this report’s generation.

52
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:11,299
N/A is missing usable timing
information, not a zero-minute arrival.

53
00:03:11,649 --> 00:03:14,240
Preserve that distinction
when explaining averages.

54
00:03:14,470 --> 00:03:18,880
Call Unit Times answers a more
specific question: which dispatch

55
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:22,450
and status timestamps exist
for each unit on each call?

56
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:26,599
Its parameter form has start
and end times and Run Report.

57
00:03:27,019 --> 00:03:30,219
Here the range starts October
twenty-fourth at twenty-three

58
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:33,900
thirty and ends October
twenty-ninth at twenty-three thirty.

59
00:03:34,309 --> 00:03:37,510
That begins thirty minutes earlier
than our first Call Summary.

60
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:41,029
Keep those periods distinct
when reconciling the results.

61
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:46,080
The generated report has Dispatched,
En Route, On Scene, Staging,

62
00:03:46,279 --> 00:03:48,409
Cleared and Linked By columns.

63
00:03:48,859 --> 00:03:53,510
The original DEMO rows say Dispatch
only and leave later status times blank.

64
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:57,019
A dispatch entry alone does
not establish an arrival.

65
00:03:57,460 --> 00:03:59,929
Other exercise rows say Unit status.

66
00:04:00,370 --> 00:04:04,460
Read the recorded timestamps as they
stand; some are incomplete or out

67
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:08,749
of order, so review the underlying
history before calculating a duration.

68
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:13,180
Some calls explicitly report no
unit dispatch or status data.

69
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:18,050
The native exercise shows Ambulance
61 with dispatch and en-route times,

70
00:04:18,219 --> 00:04:20,029
while its later cells remain blank.

71
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:24,820
These are software records, not
independent physical-arrival verification.

72
00:04:25,149 --> 00:04:30,179
Use Download CSV on this report to obtain
its unit rows for the same parameters.

73
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:35,510
The downloaded file includes call and unit
identifiers, timestamps and Linked By.

74
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:39,750
This exercise export contains
thirteen unit rows for twelve

75
00:04:39,750 --> 00:04:44,370
distinct calls: a call with two
units creates two rows, while a call

76
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:46,380
with no unit rows contributes none.

77
00:04:46,790 --> 00:04:50,449
The file uses DispatchOnly and
Explicit codes where the screen

78
00:04:50,449 --> 00:04:52,939
says Dispatch only and Unit status.

79
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:57,120
Keep blank times blank, and retain
the report period with your file.

80
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:01,700
For a separate comparison, the parameters
now show the default period spanning

81
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:05,559
late December twenty twenty-five
to late December twenty twenty-six.

82
00:05:05,939 --> 00:05:08,099
This is not the earlier October week.

83
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:11,599
Select Command in Group
and use Run Report.

84
00:05:12,059 --> 00:05:15,859
The result explicitly says Command,
including the groups beneath it.

85
00:05:16,249 --> 00:05:18,659
It contains five calls and none open.

86
00:05:19,030 --> 00:05:23,859
Its detail table shows those five exercise
rows with Command dispatch attribution.

87
00:05:24,189 --> 00:05:27,960
A group filter narrows the report;
it does not move resources,

88
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:29,750
reassign calls or close them.

89
00:05:29,979 --> 00:05:33,289
The longer period also
produces Yearly Statistics.

90
00:05:33,739 --> 00:05:37,470
An asterisk marks a partial
period; the note explains that

91
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:39,319
the report covers only part of it.

92
00:05:39,789 --> 00:05:42,869
Do not describe that row as
a complete calendar year.

93
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:47,809
Quarterly Statistics adds YTD, the
cumulative call count within the

94
00:05:47,809 --> 00:05:50,090
report’s included periods for that year.

95
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:54,510
Monthly Statistics uses the
same Calls and YTD distinction.

96
00:05:54,989 --> 00:05:56,999
October contains five calls.

97
00:05:57,370 --> 00:06:01,729
November and December show zero
calls while YTD remains five.

98
00:06:02,169 --> 00:06:05,870
These are results from available
records in the chosen range, not

99
00:06:05,870 --> 00:06:07,620
forecasts of future activity.

100
00:06:07,810 --> 00:06:12,029
Return to parameters for the same
default period and choose All Groups.

101
00:06:12,450 --> 00:06:16,260
Keep both time boundaries
unchanged, then Run Report.

102
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:21,260
This result contains sixteen
calls: four open and twelve closed.

103
00:06:21,659 --> 00:06:25,569
That can be compared with the five-call
Command result because the periods match.

104
00:06:25,899 --> 00:06:29,530
It should not be treated as a new
duplicate merely because the earlier

105
00:06:29,530 --> 00:06:31,290
October week contained fifteen.

106
00:06:31,729 --> 00:06:35,620
Establish the window and scope before
interpreting a change in totals.

107
00:06:35,810 --> 00:06:39,950
Chicago Fire can review station
call distribution; community care

108
00:06:39,979 --> 00:06:43,700
and mental-health teams can review
operational workload without putting

109
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:45,699
client histories into report notes.

110
00:06:46,079 --> 00:06:49,119
Search-and-rescue teams can
compare mission-period groups.

111
00:06:49,510 --> 00:06:53,440
Security teams can inspect recorded
staffing before a patrol review,

112
00:06:53,690 --> 00:06:57,260
while wildland coordinators can
compare seasonal reporting windows.

113
00:06:57,610 --> 00:07:02,200
Industrial teams can examine unit status
chronology during a software exercise.

114
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:07,380
In each setting, choose the question,
fix the period and scope, inspect the

115
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:11,969
detail rows, and explain missing data
before sharing an authorized result.

